{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg9P27KipoYcXwTk6zwEV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Korean vs. English token counts\n\nI couldn't browse in this session, so the figures below come from my training knowledge. Check the links before you rely on any number.\n\n## OpenAI\n\n**Source:** OpenAI, *\"Hello GPT-4o\"* (May 2024), \"Language tokenization\" section: https://openai.com/index/hello-gpt-4o/\n\nOpenAI compared its older and newer tokenizers using the same sample sentence translated into each language:\n\n| Tokenizer | English | Korean | Korean ÷ English |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `cl100k_base` (GPT-4 / GPT-3.5) | 27 | 45 | **~1.67×** (~67% more) |\n| `o200k_base` (GPT-4o and later) | 24 | 27 | **~1.13×** (~13% more) |\n\nOpenAI described this as Korean needing \"1.7x fewer tokens\" with the new tokenizer. That figure comes from a single sentence, so treat it as an illustration, not a corpus average.\n\n**Broader study:** Petrov et al., *\"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages\"* (NeurIPS 2023, arXiv:2305.15425). They measured the \"tokenization premium\" (tokens relative to English) on the FLORES-200 parallel corpus for several tokenizers, including `cl100k_base`. Korean has a clear premium there, but I don't trust my memory of the exact figure, so please read it from the paper's tables.\n\n## Claude (Anthropic)\n\nI don't know of any official Anthropic figure for Korean versus English. Anthropic's docs explain token counting and pricing, but they don't publish ratios for each language. Third-party blog posts generally report that Claude's tokenizers use noticeably more tokens for Korean than for English. Those numbers vary with the model generation and the text, and I can't confirm a specific one without a source in front of me.\n\nThe reliable way to get a number is to measure it yourself. Anthropic's token-counting endpoint (`POST /v1/messages/count_tokens`, documented at https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/token-counting) returns exact counts for a given model.\n\n## Practical takeaway\n\n- **OpenAI, GPT-4o-era tokenizer:** Korean costs roughly 10–20% more tokens than equivalent English. With the older GPT-4 tokenizer it was about 1.5–2×.\n- **Claude:** there's no published figure, so measure it with `count_tokens` on your own parallel text.\n\nI can write a small script that counts tokens for a set of parallel Korean/English sentences (for example, from FLORES-200) using `tiktoken` (`cl100k_base` and `o200k_base`) and Anthropic's `count_tokens` API. It would need your Anthropic API key, and the sample text would be sent to Anthropic's API."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2456,"cache_read_input_tokens":535,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2456},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34984cb7-be32-4166-8dac-bd11cb73ad1a","uuid":"ad7d3e67-5db7-455d-93e0-bc3ed7c1cf03","timestamp":"2026-10-18T08:23:42.955Z","request_id":"req_011Cg9P275q7Bz7qy5KBEyXs"}
